A `.env.example` file exists specifically so it can be safely committed to git as a template with placeholder values. This one has what appears to be an actual project URL and an actual service-role key (the `sb_secret_` prefix matches Supabase's current key format). **A service-role key bypasses every RLS policy you've built — all of it.** If this is real, treat it as compromised the moment it left your machine (including having been pasted into this chat). **Go rotate it in the Supabase dashboard right now** (Project Settings → API → regenerate service role key), regardless of how confident you are that it's fake or already replaced elsewhere. Then make sure the real `.env` is in `.gitignore` and `.env.example` only ever contains placeholder text like `your-service-role-key-here`. This is a five-minute fix and it's the single highest-priority item in this entire document.

**2. The backend will not boot on Render as configured.**
`backend/app.py` does `from backend.agent_graph import ...`, and `backend/agent_graph.py` does `from backend.state import SahayakState` — both treating `backend` as an importable Python *package* from one level above it. But your `render.yaml` runs `uvicorn app:app` (not `uvicorn backend.app:app`), and your `Dockerfile` sets `WORKDIR /app`, copies the `backend/` folder's contents directly into it, and runs `uvicorn app:app` from there too. In both cases, the process's working directory *is* the folder these files live in — there's no `backend/` package reachable from that location. This will fail immediately on startup with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'backend'`. **Fix:** either change the imports to relative/flat (`from agent_graph import ...`, `from state import SahayakState`) to match how the app is actually being run, or run it as `uvicorn backend.app:app` from the repo root and set Render's root directory accordingly — pick one and make it consistent. While you're in there: the Dockerfile and `render.yaml` currently specify two different deployment mechanisms (`env: python` in `render.yaml` tells Render to ignore the Dockerfile and use its native Python buildpack instead) — decide on one and delete the other, so there's no ambiguity about which actually runs.

**3. The internal-secret check is inverted — `/run` is currently unauthenticated on the public internet.**
```python
if INTERNAL_SECRET and x_sahayak_internal_secret:
    if x_sahayak_internal_secret != INTERNAL_SECRET:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=403, ...)
```
Read this closely: if a request arrives with **no** `X-Sahayak-Internal-Secret` header at all, `x_sahayak_internal_secret` is `None`, the outer `if` is `False`, and the whole check is skipped — the request is allowed through with zero authentication. Anyone who finds your Render URL (these are guessable/discoverable, e.g. `sahayak-ai-backend.onrender.com`) can call `POST /run` with any `citizen_id` they want, trigger orchestration runs, burn your Groq quota, and write `agent_events`/`applications` rows attributed to a citizen they don't control — no secret required. **Fix:** require the header unconditionally — `if x_sahayak_internal_secret != INTERNAL_SECRET: raise HTTPException(403)`, full stop, no "if present" gate. Also drop the hardcoded fallback (`os.getenv("INTERNAL_SHARED_SECRET", "sahayak-internal-secret-2026")`) — a secret with a public, guessable default value defeats the purpose the moment the real env var isn't set; fail loudly on startup instead if it's missing.

**4. The Edge Function silently impersonates the demo citizen on any auth failure.**
```ts
let citizenId = "d0000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"; // Rahul Sharma
if (authHeader) {
  const { data: { user } } = await supabaseAdmin.auth.getUser(token);
  if (user?.id) citizenId = user.id;
